  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「ハゼノキ」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%83%8F%E3%82%BC%E3%83%8E%E3%82%AD",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 109200195,
      "text": "ハゼノキ は、ウルシ科ウルシ属の落葉小高木。単にハゼとも言う。東南アジアから東アジアの温暖な地域に自生する。秋に美しく紅葉することで知られ、ウルシほどではないがかぶれることもある。日本には、果実から木蝋 を採取する資源作物として、江戸時代頃に琉球王国から持ち込まれた。昭和30年代まではポマードやチックなどへの需要があったが、その後急速に需要が減少した。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Toxicodendron succedaneum」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toxicodendron_succedaneum",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1225112718,
      "text": "Toxicodendron succedaneum, the wax tree, Japanese Hazenoki tree, sơn in Vietnamese or charão in Portuguese, is a flowering plant species in the genus Toxicodendron found in Asia, although it has been planted elsewhere, most notably Australia and New Zealand. It is a large shrub or tree, up to 8 m tall, somewhat similar to a sumac tree. Because of its beautiful autumn foliage, it has been planted outside Asia as an ornamental plant, often by gardeners who were apparently unaware of the dangers of allergic reactions. It is now officially classified as a noxious weed in Australia and New Zealand. It is one of the city tree symbols of Kurume, Fukuoka, Japan."
    }
  },
